ngo  and Walla and I thought this is very  interesting whenever we talk about  immigration between the u.s. and Mexico  we always talking about Mexicans coming  to the United States we don't ever think  about why would Americans go to Mexico  although of course if you're thinking  about eight the 1890s so this is one one  year before Plessy vs. Ferguson the  separate-but-equal ruling by the Supreme  Court and just the presence of Jim Crow  there's lots of reasons why  african-americans would actually find  Mexico a potentially attractive place to  be and so I got very interested in  thinking about this sort of hidden other  migration that we don't think about and  I got very interested in once I was  looking through the records at trying to  figure out who was behind this who did  who would realize that with the new  train system between the u.s. and Mexico  that it didn't necessarily only need to  go from north to south that actually  could go from south to north and it was  a very mysterious figure who went by  these various names of William Ellis or  Giamatti sale and that's the initial  spark that got me interested in trying  to figure out who was this person who  had this very interesting vision of the  border and what the relationship between  the US and Mexico could be  today we're located downtown at the main  branch of the San Antonio Public Library  not far from where Ellis lived and  worked since you spent so much time  immersing yourself in 19th and early  20th century San Antonio life as you  research the book I'd like you to take  us back there for a moment what was this  place like then that's a great question  you know it's in really one thing that's  really fascinating is that San Antonio  was his hub of trade across the border  into into Mexico connected particularly  to Monterrey and so I don't think it's  at all accidental that William Ellis  ends up moving here there's a couple  things that are that are happening so he  grows up in Victoria you 