ese  tricks are stories which were really  central to folklore from slavery and its  aftermath in some ways I think informed  his perspective on the world but that is  a sort of leap of faith that I'm making  and trying to contextualize his cultural  background as I read your book he struck  me as someone who could easily be a  character in the work of say James  Weldon Johnson  and because he deploys some of the  strategies that Johnson himself in his  own autobiography confesses to deploying  when he would occasionally pass his Cuba  mm-hmm what were Ellis's strategies for  for passing you you touched on some of  them but I where if you could spell this  out a little bit more yeah it's it's  fascinating that's a great question  because it I think we think about  passing is purely about phenotype and  and there's a little bit of that of  course and that he's he's relatively  light skinned and so that allows him to  say that he would be Mexican and if you  can look at the cover the photo he also  sort of cultivates one of these big  Mexican style mustaches but there's a  there's a lot more to just your visit  than your physical appearance that's  involved so part of it is of course  dress and the way that he dresses  invokes a very upper-class form of  mexicana dad that's very much sort of in  touch with European models that are very  influential in Mexico at this time  period and language is a big part of it  as well so that there's a great line  from Langston Hughes that I quote in the  book and James Weldon Johnson does the  same thing so that when you have to go  buy your ticket on the railroad and the  railroads are one of the places that are  segregated during this time period you  buy your ticket in Spanish and the  ticket taker will often make the  assumption because there's really no way  to prove what your race is you know at  this time has a driver's license or  passport or anything so the ticket taker  will make an assumption of this person  who talked t